praetorian
/prəˈtôrēən/
Praetorian most commonly refers to the Praetorian Guard, an elite unit of the Imperial Roman army whose members served as personal bodyguards and intelligence agents for Roman emperors. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praetorian_Guard), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxen8Ln5nNQ&t=61)

The Praetorian Guard in Ancient Rome

• Origins: Established in 27 BCE by Emperor Augustus, the force evolved from temporary Republican bodyguards known as cohors praetoria. [1] (https://historycrunch.com/praetorian-guard/), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxen8Ln5nNQ&t=61)
• Privileges & Pay: Praetorians received significantly higher pay (over three times the rate of a regular legionary), served shorter terms (12 to 16 years compared to 20 for regular legions), and were stationed directly inside or near Rome. [1] (https://www.facebook.com/ancienthistoryexplorers/videos/the-praetorian-guard-was-the-elite-unit-of-soldiers-who-served-as-the-personal-b/2552093281808020/), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/shorts/FurnmG0yV44), [3] (https://historycrunch.com/praetorian-guard/)
• Political Power: Due to their close proximity to the throne, the Guard wielded massive influence. They frequently made and unbroke emperors, assassinated rulers such as Caligula and Pertinax, and famously auctioned off the imperial throne to Didius Julianus in 193 CE. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SCFrVBvtKXA&t=77), [2] (https://www.facebook.com/ancienthistoryexplorers/videos/the-praetorian-guard-began-as-an-elite-unit-created-by-augustus-the-first-empero/26013316968364186/), [3] (https://www.facebook.com/ancienthistoryexplorers/videos/the-praetorian-guard-was-originally-created-by-augustus-as-an-elite-force-tasked/1447731169804659/), [4] (https://historycrunch.com/praetorian-guard/)
• Disbandment: Emperor Constantine I permanently dissolved and disbanded the Praetorian Guard in the early 4th century (312 CE) after they supported his rival Maxentius at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge, razing their fortress, the Castra Praetoria. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praetorian_Guard), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SCFrVBvtKXA&t=77)

Modern Meanings and Uses

• Adjective: Used to describe something resembling an imperial bodyguard, or (more broadly) a corrupt, politically venal, or self-interested elite force. [1] (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/praetorian), [2] (https://www.vocabulary.com/dictionary/Praetorian)
• Cybersecurity: Praetorian is an American continuous offensive security and adversarial AI platform that helps enterprises find and patch vulnerabilities. [1] (https://www.praetorian.com/), [2] (https://www.linkedin.com/company/praetorian)
• Arts and Media: The term appears frequently in historical fiction, strategy games (such as the Praetorian video game), and popular culture (e.g., enemy archetypes in the Star Wars or Mad Max universes). [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praetorian)
